# Line-ending policy for Osvanta Bus Log Analyzer.
#
# Without this file, what git does with line endings depends on each machine's
# `core.autocrlf` setting. That is why the repository looks clean on Windows
# (autocrlf=true) but reports every text file as modified when the same working
# tree is read from a Linux VM or container (autocrlf unset). This file removes
# the ambiguity: the rules below travel with the repository and apply
# identically on every machine, CI runner and contributor clone.
#
# The repository already stores LF for every text file, so adding this causes
# no churn -- `git add --renormalize .` produces zero changes.

# Default: git decides what is text, normalises it to LF in the repository, and
# checks it out using the platform's native convention.
*   text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scripts, where the line ending is functional rather than cosmetic
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# cmd.exe can mis-handle LF-only batch files -- `goto`, labels and multi-line
# blocks are the usual casualties. Pin these to CRLF regardless of platform.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and git hooks are run by sh/bash; a CR at the end of the
# shebang line makes them fail with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh            text eol=lf
tools/hooks/*   text eol=lf
tests/hooks/*   text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test fixtures
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned to LF so every machine sees byte-identical fixtures. Parser tests that
# depend on offsets, lengths or checksums then behave the same everywhere.
# Note: do NOT mark these `-text`. That would tell git to store the working-tree
# bytes verbatim, which on a Windows checkout means committing CRLF into
# fixtures that are currently LF.
tests/fixtures/**   text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary -- never normalise, never diff as text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.icns   binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.pyd    binary

# Measurement and log formats. BLF, MF4 and MDF are binary container formats;
# a line-ending conversion would corrupt them. None are tracked today, but
# fixtures get added over time and the failure would be silent.
*.blf    binary
*.mf4    binary
*.mdf    binary
*.dat    binary

# ASC and CSV logs, DBC and ARXML databases are text. They are left to
# `text=auto` above, which keeps them LF in the repository.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Diff and review quality-of-life
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.py     diff=python
*.md     diff=markdown

# Generated or vendored files: keep them out of diffs and language statistics.
licenses/**    linguist-vendored
CHANGELOG.md   merge=union
